@LunarVVolf Believe or disbelieve whatever you like, but the fact remains that anyone running the majority of the economy b450 motherboards would be much better served upgrading to a (currently) very affordable, better quality b550...ESPECIALLY if they're running a Zen 2 or better processor (or in some cases, a higher end Zen+.)

You'll get more out of the hardware you've already paid for; motherboard quality matters.

Putting a new generation video card on a b450 that you bought as cheaply as possible 3-4 years ago is akin to puting $5000 of chrome on a Ford Pinto with two burned valves.

@LunarVVolf What dinosaur told you "the only time to upgrade a mainboard is when you upgrade processors"?  That stopped remotely being true with Socket AM4 and Windows 10; hell, even Intel processors will now accept a board change on Win10 with no muss, fuss, formatting, or redo of setup...you change the board and you're up and running.

And I didn't address what you said about the 6800 because it absolutely doesn't matter in the slightest.  If you're running a b450 and a Zen+ or lower tier Zen 2 processor, your bottleneck is going to be at the processor (unless you're still running a GTX 760) no matter if you're running on 8x lanes or 16x.  

PCIe speeds aren't created equal because motherboards and materials aren't created equal, but a better board stands a better chance of getting you better speeds because of better materials, so unless you're running a (what was at the time) high-dollar top of the line b450 (in which case, you probably aren't looking for the lowest tier graphics card possible because you just aren't in that market segment) then an upgrade to an overstocked and currently underpriced b550 is likely to help you across all performance metrics...not just gaming.  Couple that with the rising availability of "used" 5600x and 5800x processors that are starting to hit the secondary market as people like me finally get the 5900x and 5950x processors we wanted/needed in the first place (processors that a b450 cannot utilize) and the fact that literally every other component involved will carry forward to both the more efficient board and (should you choose to upgrade) a Zen 3 processor, and you'd be a fool not to maximize your system output for very minimal investment no matter which video card you end up getting.

 @LePedant  Sadly, laptops don't really count.

Their (extremely short) E-Waste timer starts ticking before you even open the box.

If you have to have one for a business application they're fine, but I've never understood people who purchase them for home use since Planned Obsolescence is baked into every bevel and screw, and the ultimate goal of the manufacturer is to give you just enough machine that you HAVE to replace it in a 3-4 year span (unlike a decent, non-Dell desktop which can be kept running at a reasonable speed for close to a decade with some not-very-expensive upgrades.)

 @Rspsand07  well, it's not a bad idea to do it like that. Exclusivity for FSR would kill the idea as it pales against DLSS and even if it survived it would lead to the same mess of Nvidia or AMD optimized titles, which Nvidia will be much more numerous with that.

What I meant by "FSR good" is that in many games that implement it and DLSS, AMD will at least be mostly competitive in image quality and performance instead having to sacrafice performance to merely get similar but slightly better looks to Nvidia, with no way before FSR to get similar fieldity without being far less performant than Nvidia with DLSS.

Basically, tldr, FSR is open so it can be adopted as a general standard so many games will at worst for AMD implement both FSR and DLSS and AMD won't be as blown out of the competition by Nvidia because of DLSS, as a good-enough alternatuve in the form of FSR is available for AMD. With added benefits that other GPUs can use the same tech too.

 @svn5994  The Series X's CPU is closest to the 3700X - it has less cache, but the hardware decompression build into the IO for the SSD saves it a load of CPU overhead which PCs don't get to have. The OS is also less overhead than windows, and developers can optimise knowing that every console will have an NVME SSD.

PC specs on paper will need to be more powerful to compensate. Also the Series X graphics processor is way more powerful than a 1060, don't kid yourself.

As for native 4K - who actually cares. I know I don't. The benefits above 1440p are about edge clarity on screens with the pixel count to do that. Surface detail? Colour gradation? You need to get up close to squint to see it, and I don't care about what needs a magnifying glass to notice.

 @roundduckgirl  yeah I was speaking in particular against the 6600XT

I think you can generally cut the GPUs that'll stack up well against the consoles in the long haul vs the ones that won't based on how evident the shortage was around the release of the product.

Eg. the 3060Ti's biggest handicap is VRAM but aside from that it'll have plenty of compute to keep it chugging along for a good long time. The base RTX 3060 in comparison, has the opposite problem, and because of that it's surplus of VRAM is mostly useless. It'll still be "fine" for a while, but it'll be outperformed by the consoles.

